{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"caption\":\"Tekoh v. County of Los Angeles (Tekoh II)\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2023-08-04\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"75 F.4th 1264; No. 18-56414\",\"proposition\":\"On remand from Vega, held that excluding qualified expert testimony about minimization, false-evidence ploys, stress-compliant confessions, and false-confession phenomena was an abuse of discretion under Rule 702; such testimony can assist jurors without impermissibly vouching for the defendant. The 2024 rehearing opinions identified tension with United States v. Benally, 541 F.3d 990 (10th Cir. 2008).\",\"doctrine_node\":\"voluntariness/false-confession experts/Vega remand\",\"discovery_path\":\"Vega progeny and false-confession-expert searches -> Ninth Circuit merits opinion -> 2024 published rehearing opinions and Benally split discussion\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"Tekoh v. County of Los Angeles (denial of rehearing en banc)\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-01-25\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 18-56414\",\"proposition\":\"Published order denied panel and en banc rehearing of Tekoh II. The concurrence characterized the Rule 702 holding as narrow; the dissent argued it improperly constitutionalized admission of coerced-confession experts and created a circuit conflict with the Tenth Circuit's Benally rule.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"voluntariness/false-confession experts/circuit-split signal\",\"discovery_path\":\"Vega progeny query -> official Ninth Circuit rehearing order -> concurrence and dissent reviewed for split and limiting language\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"Bowen v. Secretary, Florida Department of Corrections\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-02-15\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-11744\",\"proposition\":\"Under AEDPA, a fairminded jurist could conclude that officers did not interrogate a sixteen-year-old suspect merely by placing him with a codefendant in a monitored interrogation room after he invoked counsel, where the codefendant was not an agent and police employed no coercive psychological ploy; youth remained part of the totality but was not dispositive.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"juvenile confessions/Edwards invocation/Innis functional equivalent\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 juvenile Miranda and functional-equivalent searches -> Eleventh Circuit published opinion -> AEDPA posture and Arizona v. Mauro analysis checked\",\"confidence\":0.97}\n{\"caption\":\"Grimes v. Phillips\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-06-26\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 21-56353\",\"proposition\":\"Under AEDPA, Supreme Court precedent does not clearly establish that using an undercover jail informant after a suspect invokes Miranda counsel violates Edwards; the state court reasonably applied Illinois v. Perkins because a suspect unaware that he is speaking to police does not face the police-dominated coercion Miranda addresses.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Edwards invocation/undercover informants/AEDPA\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 Edwards and undercover-informant searches -> Ninth Circuit FOR PUBLICATION opinion -> Perkins and clearly-established-law analysis checked\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Campbell\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-08-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-3283\",\"proposition\":\"A parole condition requiring truthful reporting and responses did not impose an unconstitutional penalty for invoking the Fifth Amendment because invocation itself could constitute a response. A routine, unannounced home visit by familiar armed parole officers was noncustodial under Miranda's objective totality test; parole status alone does not establish custody.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Miranda custody/parole supervision/penalty doctrine\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 parole Miranda custody and compelled-answer searches -> Seventh Circuit precedential opinion -> Minnesota v. Murphy and custody analyses checked\",\"confidence\":0.96}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Peña\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-09-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"115 F.4th 1254; No. 23-2047\",\"proposition\":\"Held a confession voluntary despite a nearly four-hour interrogation, raised voices, emotionally charged references to the suspect's children and former wife, and suggestions that cooperation had benefited others. The tactics were troubling and uncivil, but did not constitute sufficiently coercive promises or overbear the suspect's will under the totality.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"voluntariness/emotional pressure/cooperation inducements\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 federal confession-voluntariness searches -> Tenth Circuit published opinion -> later Perez opinion's treatment of Peña cross-checked\",\"confidence\":0.94}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Little\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-10-11\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"119 F.4th 750; No. 23-5077\",\"proposition\":\"The statement that the suspect wanted to talk to a lawyer 'to see where I stood' while saying he could still talk was an ambiguous reference to counsel, so Davis did not require questioning to stop or officers to clarify. Davis superseded contrary aspects of the circuit's pre-Davis Giles precedent. False-evidence claims, references to separation from the suspect's son, and punishment discussions did not render the statements involuntary.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Miranda invocation/ambiguity/Davis limitation of circuit precedent\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 ambiguous counsel invocation searches -> Tenth Circuit PUBLISH opinion -> Giles limitation and voluntariness holdings checked\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Martinez\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-11-19\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-2193\",\"proposition\":\"A written Miranda waiver obtained before a polygraph examination remained effective for the immediately ensuing post-test interview. The polygraph did not itself make the warnings stale, and no renewed warnings were required absent a significant break, changed circumstances, or an objectively apparent limitation of the waiver to the test phase.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Miranda waiver/polygraph/post-test interrogation/staleness\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 polygraph Miranda waiver searches -> Tenth Circuit published opinion -> pretest and post-test continuity analysis checked\",\"confidence\":0.98}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Perez\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-01-17\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-3015\",\"proposition\":\"Upheld an implied Miranda waiver and confession where agents accurately presented the real choice between cooperation and likely imprisonment. Conditional or limited assurances, family references, false-evidence tactics, urgency, profanity, and discussion of sentencing did not individually or cumulatively overbear the suspect's will; his refusal to provide the requested cooperation reinforced voluntariness.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Miranda implied waiver/voluntariness/interrogation tactics\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Miranda waiver and coercive-tactics searches -> Tenth Circuit PUBLISH opinion -> cited Peña and Little boundaries cross-checked\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Purks\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-06-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-4495\",\"proposition\":\"Assuming state prison guards previously beat the suspect, the coercion did not taint his later DEA confession. Applying carryover factors drawn from Oregon v. Elstad—including elapsed time, intervening events, different interrogators and sovereigns, and the later interview's tone—the court held the prior coercion's effects had dissipated; the opinion supplied the circuit's first detailed guidance on that question.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"voluntariness/attenuation/prior physical coercion\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 coerced-confession attenuation searches -> Fourth Circuit PUBLISHED opinion -> Elstad carryover-factor analysis checked\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Morgan\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-07-11\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-11114\",\"proposition\":\"An agent violated Miranda by asking about phone ownership after an unequivocal invocation, but the voluntary answer could be considered when deciding Fourth Amendment abandonment, and physical evidence from the phone was not suppressible under Patane and Vega. The agent's misleading statement that she was not trying to interrogate the suspect was factual deception, not the kind of deception about Miranda's legal guarantees condemned in Beale, Hart, and Lall.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Vega progeny/Miranda physical fruits/abandonment/deception\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Vega Tekoh progeny and post-invocation physical-fruit searches -> Eleventh Circuit PUBLISH opinion -> Patane, Vega, Beale, Hart, and Lall treatment checked\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"Hernandez v. McIntosh\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-07-21\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-1816\",\"proposition\":\"Affirmed conditional habeas relief where seven hours of unwarned questioning were followed immediately by warnings and a repeated confession. The state trial court's categorical instruction that later confessions need not be disregarded even if the first was involuntary was contrary to or an unreasonable application of Missouri v. Seibert; the immediate repetition required exclusion, while a still-later prosecutor interview required attenuation and curative-measure analysis.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Seibert two-step interrogation/voluntariness/habeas\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Seibert and successive-confession searches -> Second Circuit precedential opinion -> jury instruction, immediate repetition, and later-interview analyses checked\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"O.W. v. Carr\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-04-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-1288\",\"proposition\":\"A thirteen-year-old student's confession during questioning by an assistant principal was voluntary under the ordinary totality test: age required consideration but did not mandate a school-specific rule, and the familiar setting, sub-two-hour duration, absence of deprivation or threats, and truthful description of consequences did not overbear his will. Under Vega, failure to administer Miranda warnings alone supplied no Section 1983 claim.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"juvenile confessions/school interrogation/voluntariness/Vega civil remedy\",\"discovery_path\":\"2026 juvenile school-confession and Vega searches -> Fourth Circuit PUBLISHED opinion -> age, school setting, voluntariness, and Section 1983 holdings checked\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Chapman\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-05-08\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-4939\",\"proposition\":\"A suspect with autism, Tourette syndrome, ADHD, OCD, and developmental delays knowingly and intelligently waived Miranda where he signed a waiver, repeatedly demonstrated awareness of his rights, had prior federal criminal-justice experience, and said he understood. General statements that officers would help him and that honesty goes a long way did not promise sufficient leniency to overbear his will.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Miranda waiver/neurodivergence/voluntariness/leniency suggestions\",\"discovery_path\":\"2026 mental-capacity Miranda and voluntariness searches -> Ninth Circuit FOR PUBLICATION opinion -> waiver factors and leniency analysis checked\",\"confidence\":0.99}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Bendann\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-06-25\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 25-4033\",\"proposition\":\"Officers did not engage in Miranda interrogation by silently presenting an iPhone after an early-morning SWAT search; under the court's five-question framework, the suspect's reflexive entry of his passcode was an unprompted voluntary act rather than a response officers should have known was reasonably likely to elicit an incriminating testimonial statement.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Miranda interrogation/Innis functional equivalent/device passcodes\",\"discovery_path\":\"2026 Miranda interrogation and phone-passcode searches -> Fourth Circuit PUBLISHED opinion -> five-question framework and silent-presentation facts checked\",\"confidence\":0.99}"}}
